Z||000004||Adrift||11-30-2001||10:05 AM||||If you're gona invest your money somewhere, invest it in brakes! (At least thats what I have always thought!)<P>What price did you get on those? I'm looking to upgrade my break system and am curious about the costs involved<BR>Thanks<BR>Ari<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Adrift (edited 11-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||SwissFormula||11-30-2001||10:30 AM||||It's not a fault to invest in better brakes, imo there has to be a balance between power, handling and brakes -&gt; you do the right thing for that north*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Roy<P>88 Formula, T-Top<BR>1 of 20 Fieros in Switzerland||||reg||14
Z||000006||artherd||11-30-2001||03:36 PM||||Hi man, they're pricy... $1900shiped for *just* calipers and 13" outter rotors. (still need pads, lines, fluid, etc.) Most important, I still need to fabricate the caliper mounting brackets.  That's a one-off custom job, so it'll probally cost me another grand. Total will be north of $3k.<P>Held Motorsports sells a fairly comprable 13" setup (using infact lighter Wilwood calipers) for $1500 per end of your car! (he just split the ends, so now you can upgrade the fronts-only pretty darn cheap!)<P>Held's setup front and rear, installed, (his kit is *complete*) will cost less than mine by the time I'm done.   <P>You can install his kit in a weekend, if you can change pads on a stock car, you can install the Held 13"-ers! (and when you do, the pads are even easier to change, you don't even have to remove the caliper at all! Infact, if you have 18" or bigger open wheels, you don't even have to remove the *wheel* to change the pads, cool  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I think my setup will be able to brake a notch harder than his, and I am doing several other things to the car that make the suspension look pretty non-stock anyway, so I went the 993T caliper route. (plus I just enjoy good old fassioned quality time with a 6-axis mill!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR>PS: my thoughts exactly, an engine won't save my life just by being good, but brakes *WILL*!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>PS: Roger, to each, his own! That's what makes this world so much fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Adrift:<BR><B>If you're gona invest your money somewhere, invest it in brakes! (At least thats what I have always thought!)<P>What price did you get on those? I'm looking to upgrade my break system and am curious about the costs involved<BR>Thanks<BR>Ari<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Adrift (edited 11-30-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000007||FieroLisa||11-30-2001||04:03 PM||||no, you're crazy...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>One phrase comes to mind..."psycho hose beast..."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||avengador1||11-30-2001||04:13 PM||||With those you should be able to stop on a dime and get nine cents change. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||11-30-2001||07:33 PM||||If you want to get the most out of those brakes(btw,nice hardware there)I would consider having some hubs machined,as the stock ones are cast,very weak.<BR>That way you can use some larger bolts for the wheels instead of the studs.<BR>The spindles are steel,and can be welded,machined if necessary.<BR>If you can measure your setup in relation to the mounting ears on the spindle,you could probably find an existing bracket that can be used with only minor work,just an idea.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Pheagey||12-01-2001||01:08 AM||||&lt;Pheagey leave before he starts crying&gt;<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed;<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects/pheageysfeiro<BR>"The car formaly know as, and has now reverted back to being known as, Pheagey's Fiero."||||reg||9
Z||000011||SKIDMARK||12-01-2001||01:20 AM||||What good is a car that doesn't stop? Personally, I prefer "go" to "stop" any day, but what do I know. $1900 incomplete brakes do seem a bit....uh... excentric. I squirm if I have to buy rotors when I do a brake job.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> Wudda ya doin with the old brakes?<p>[This message has been edited by SKIDMARK (edited 12-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-01-2001||08:19 AM||||Its gonna be a lot of work to put them on.. so being the nice guy I am, I will save you the hassle of putting them on and buy them off you for $50 and a half eaten chocolate bar  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>PS  they look great!||||reg||6
Z||000013||artherd||12-03-2001||02:59 AM||||That's actually exactly what I'm doing :P)  The new hubs are off a Pontiac 6000 (90mm outer bearing carrier veruss the 72mm stock 88 rear bearing, the bigger of the two!)<P>I belive that on the 88s the hubs are machined steel, and I know they're actually quite strong.<P>But I am having the 6000 parts split, worked to convert the outter hub piece to a 5x130mm wheel bolt setup (with threads cut into the hub, which is going to end up being about 30mm thick now)   Then the new assy will be pressed back into the 6000's 90mm SKF bearings.<P>This car's taking a while to build (money trickles in), but boy is it fun!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>If you want to get the most out of those brakes(btw,nice hardware there)I would consider having some hubs machined,as the stock ones are cast,very weak.<BR>That way you can use some larger bolts for the wheels instead of the studs.<BR>The spindles are steel,and can be welded,machined if necessary.<BR>If you can measure your setup in relation to the mounting ears on the spindle,you could probably find an existing bracket that can be used with only minor work,just an idea.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000014||onfire||12-03-2001||02:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>This car's taking a while to build (money trickles in), but boy is it fun!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The money trickles in, but it flows out like Niagra Falls!<P>Thats an awesome setup. I myself am going the cheap way. 11.25" with Archie's adapters. Which came in the mail friday. I only have about $500 of parts left to buy. Not including the beer and pizza for a few of my friends to help install them.<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/onfiresig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||jelly2m8||12-03-2001||04:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>That's actually exactly what I'm doing :P)  The new hubs are off a Pontiac 6000 (90mm outer bearing carrier veruss the 72mm stock 88 rear bearing, the bigger of the two!)<P>I belive that on the 88s the hubs are machined steel, and I know they're actually quite strong.<P>But I am having the 6000 parts split, worked to convert the outter hub piece to a 5x130mm wheel bolt setup (with threads cut into the hub, which is going to end up being about 30mm thick now)   Then the new assy will be pressed back into the 6000's 90mm SKF bearings.<P>This car's taking a while to build (money trickles in), but boy is it fun!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1 question, Who, or what company engineered this kit?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||88-DOHC||12-03-2001||04:56 PM||||Well, I have the Held 13" brakes (well, actually I got them before Ryane sold everything to Held). Anyway, 13" rotors + Wilwood 4 piston calipers = major stopping power.  Was it worth it? Yep! Now I can beef up the DOHC all I want, and not worry about not being able to actually stop. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||artherd||12-03-2001||06:59 PM||||Heh, tell me about it!   Why are the checks coming in so small, yet the parts sp expensive?! :S)  <P>PS: The 10.25s are a great setup for the money, they'll take your car from marginal-at-best, to hold-on-or-we'll-go-through-the-winshield! <P><BR>PS: take it from me, Budget lots for Pizza & Beer! (Then they'll come back for your engine swap  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>Jelly2m8: The engineering is being done by me (and my company) amoung many other things, I do occational light Carbonfibre aerospace parts design and fab. <BR>Actual machining will be done by a machine shop I have worked with in the past who has some crazy-neat tools. Like a $2m CNC mill in a climate controled space (makes radio telescope dishes for NASA!) <P>If these come out well, I may offer them through a friendly Fiero retailer near you!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by onfire:<BR><B> The money trickles in, but it flows out like Niagra Falls!<P>Thats an awesome setup. I myself am going the cheap way. 11.25" with Archie's adapters. Which came in the mail friday. I only have about $500 of parts left to buy. Not including the beer and pizza for a few of my friends to help install them.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
